<h2 id="faq">Gmail alternatives FAQ</h2>
<h3>What is the best Gmail alternative?</h3><p>Outlook.com is a broad alternative for Microsoft users, Proton Mail and Tuta Mail suit privacy-focused readers, Zoho Mail and Fastmail suit custom-domain needs, and iCloud Mail fits Apple users. The best choice depends on your storage, integrations, domain and migration needs.</p>
<h3>Can I keep my Gmail address if I switch apps?</h3><p>Yes. A mail app can connect to a Gmail account through a supported account type. This changes the interface, not the provider, storage limit or Google account policies.</p>
<h3>Can I move old Gmail messages to another provider?</h3><p>Usually, yes, through the destination's supported importer or a Google Takeout archive. Check messages, attachments, sent mail, contacts, calendars and labels after import. Keep the original account until the new setup is verified.</p>
<h3>Will Gmail stop supporting other email accounts?</h3><p>Google says web POP and Gmailify support for third-party accounts is being removed, with existing users supported until January 2027. The third-party Send as feature also ends in January 2027. Gmail itself is not closing.</p>
<h3>Is there a free Gmail alternative?</h3><p>Yes. Outlook.com, Proton Mail, Tuta Mail, iCloud Mail and Yahoo Mail have free entry points, but storage, account features and custom-domain support differ. Verify current limits in the official plan page for your region.</p>
<h2 id="sources">Official sources and method</h2>
<p>Product, pricing, storage, migration and support details were checked on November 1, 2026. The comparison uses official provider pages and support documents. It does not claim hands-on testing, measured deliverability, a universal privacy winner or a numerical score. For the editorial process, see the <a href="/review-methodology">review methodology</a>.</p>

